TI  - Review of the digital twin design of the smart satellite thermal control structure
T2  - ACTA ASTRONAUTICA
AB  - Intelligent thermal control structures (ITCS) have attracted growing attention as a key enabler for meeting the stringent demands of next-generation satellites in efficient heat management, autonomous regulation, and integrated system performance. With the rapid evolution of digital twin (DT) technologies, thermal control digital twins-seamlessly combining high-fidelity physical models with data-driven intelligence-are reshaping paradigms in thermal system design, in-orbit state perception, and predictive health monitoring. This paper presents a systematic review of the functional requirements and technological advancements in ITCS, encompassing passive and active thermal control materials, structure-thermal coupling mechanisms, and representative research progress. In this context, we identify and analyze the core enabling technologies underpinning DT-based thermal control, including multi-physics field modeling, hybrid reduced-order modeling, and deep learning approaches, as well as the integration of multi-source sensor data and real-time model updating mechanisms. To address current challenges in real-time adaptability, functional integration, and crossmission generalization, we propose a modular and distributed digital twin framework organized into a threelayer architecture, known as "structure-simulation-cognition", which enables dynamic thermal field mapping and intelligent control. Finally, emerging trends such as structure-function co-design, edge-intelligence deployment, and digital standardization are discussed to lay the theoretical foundation and provide engineering guidance for the next generation of intelligent satellite thermal control systems.
